I don't know how to respond to this. Normally I would compose some sort of
satire, as I started to do at the beginning (though to some radlibs, the
Trilaterals and Bilderbergers are very real). But I don't want to piss off
Jay, who may actually believe this stuff! And then again perhaps he's
having a good laugh, too.

Briefly, the various theories that one can escape income taxes, traffic
laws, littering laws, whatever, by various technicalities is not a new
idea. Libertarian and Bircher fringe types have been pushing these theories
for at least 20 years--I went to a few lectures in 1974 and even pulled the
trick of putting 20 dependents down on my W-2 form (I wised up a few months
later as I contemplated the inevitable filing of my 1040, though).

Off the top of my head, here are a few of the "tricks" that allow ordinary
citizens to evade taxes, laws, authority, etc.

* Only gold or silver (specie, or coined money) counts as real money, so
says the Constitution. When the U.S. switched to Fraudulent Reserve Notes
(or when it went off the gold standard, according to a variant of this
theory), tax liabilties vanished. (Another variant of this idea is that
gold is selling for, say, 330 Fraudulent Reserve Notes per ounce, so one
simply converts this to "dollars" as defined in the Constitution--the
Constitution said something about a dollar being 1/20th of an ounce of
gold, as I recall (you can look up the details)--and your taxable income is
reduced substantially. Your 45K income in FRS notes is "really" only 3000
dollars.)

* Ohio, I think it is, failed to correctly enter the Union...something
about how it voted, etc. (I haven't heard the details of this revelation in
nearly 20 years). Thus, all laws passed since that time, last century, are
null and void (on the theory that the U.S. is illegally constituted).

* Religions are exempt from various kinds of taxes, as we all know. And
religious types like priests and rabbis can take vows of poverty and live
off their church without paying taxes. Thus, say various religious leaders
like Reverend Kirby J. Hensley, one can create a religion, become a priest
or ayotollah, and escape taxes. (Many have become preachers in the
Universal Life Church, amongst others, hoping in some way this is true.)

* Make up your own notion of how some government edict or entity violates
something in the Constititution and hence invalidates the laws you dislike.
(Better yet, set up seminars in this theory and charge admission...just
don't let the marks see your own tax returns!)

Personally, I hate government. For me, Lysander Spooner's arguments are
enough to invalidate government (I never signed a contract, never agreed to
the founding documents of the United States, etc.....).

These whacko libertarian ideas of escaping taxes on a _technicality_ never
quite seem to work. Some of the leading tax rebels of the 1970s did their
time in jail, paid their fines, and have mostly shut up. (I'm not approving
of this, just noting the facts.)

I hope I'm wrong on this. If anybody out there knows of successful scams to
beat the system, let us know. But arguments that ""Joe hasn't been
prosecuted yet." are not enough, of course. And when I look at the civil
and criminal penalties for "failure to file," my blood runs cold--which is
I suppose just how the IRS wants me to react.

At the very least, I hope Jay P.P. reports on his experiences.

-Tim May, Skeptic





