From: Andrew S Hall <ashall@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: Slave Schools

Responding to the challenge of presenting evidence:

The problem lies not in lack of evidence, but in how you rate the
evidence. You cited the disparity of standardized test scores. That is
evidence. You also dispute whether that evidence has weight. I say that
it does, in a more limited fashion that you assumed.

The disparity of test scores simply measures that on certain tasks
Japanese, French, etc students are better. Whether those tasks are
end-all of deciding which school system is best is another matter.
The foreign schools succeed in doing what they are designe to do.
They teach students are rigorous and *set* program as well as instill
social conformity. I agree that this is _not_ an ideal, but I will
state they are successful at it. (Or more successful that their
American counterparts.)

I give the standardized test scores are fair amount of weight. They do
not measure whether our upper 10% are better than another's upper 10%.
What they measure is how the bulk of students compare. This is also
why I dissent with your statements about comparing levels in college
and grad. school. I don't disagree that a grad student in chemistry
(for example) is better off or equal to his foreign countertpart.
If he/she has had a good undergrad education and also partaken off the
more and diverse opportunities in his career, he/she is probably way ahead.
Where I disagree is if you compared a grad student in English here in the
States to a grad student in French in France. I would bet that the French
student would be more likely to score way better on the math portion of
any standardized text and probably better on the respective grammar
portion. Where Euro/Japan schools succeed is in making sure that all
students have a good grounding in the basics. That is what standardized
test measure.

I agree wholeheartedly that school choice is the way to go. I closed
my missive by saying it was not a defense of government schools. I
think we need to radically improve the state of education in the USA
and I think we should learn form what works. If I were suddenly to be
in control of a large school system (run for _my_ profit, of course)
I would probably run it very much on the French model. At any given
moment, all schools would be teaching the same thing. (Allowing for
the USA's time zones. :) ) My school system would not be for everybody,
but that it niche marketing. A lot of parents would prefer that the
school they send their child to prvide a really rigorous and hard
education in fundamentals like math, science, language, and culture.
They would then rely on themselves to teach the child creativity, dissent,
open mindedness, etc. Speaking for myself, I would not want any school,
public or private, to teach my children anything along those lines. I want
the school to teach my children the skills necessary for them to be
able to _rationally_ make up their own.

Andrew Hall

