From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: GOVT: USA: State Citizenship -> no Tax, Draft, DMV

Okay, here's a legal theory yet to be tested in the real world:

The U.S. Constitution refers in several places to 'dollars'.  In article
I, section 9, paragraph 1: ". . .not exceeding ten dollars for
each person." referring to a tax on the importation of persons (slaves).
And again in Amendment VII, twenty dollars was set as the threshold
at which trial by jury is guaranteed.  Both clauses were written
before the first coinage act (of 1793) which established the
US dollar.  What did these clauses then refer to?

The most common coin in circulation in the new United States in the
1780's and 1790's was the Spanish silver dollar.  In recognition of
this fact of commerce, the US dollar was defined in 1793 as the
average weight of the spanish dollar.  This was determined
experimentally (by weighing a sample of coins), and then written into
the law (it was about 0.78 ounces of silver).

This equation of 1 dollar= coin containing 0.78 oz silver never
varied from 1793 until the coinage was debased in 1964.  Many
reasons grounded in the constitution can be given for why the dollar
cannot be changed from the original value.  For example, the clause
in article 1, Sec. 9 para 1 about taxing the importation of slaves
was a compromise between the northern and southern states.  If the
value of the dollar could be changed, then the north could have
stopped the importation of slaves by raising the value of the
dollar until the tax was too burdensome.  Similarly, the right
to a trial by jury in civil cases could be extended by lowering
the value of the dollar, or denied by increasing the value of the
dollar.  The specific dollar amount ($20) was picked so that
trial by jury would not be invoked in small cases, but would be
available in cases where the amounts were major (remember that
$20 in the 1790s might be a month's income).

Additionally, the states, are denied both the power to coin
money and to make anthing but gold and silver coin legal tender
(Art 1, sec 10, para 1).  The congress is only granted the
power to coin money (Art 1, Sec 8, para 5).  The 10th amendment
makes clear that powers not delegated to the US are reserved
to the states or the people.  Since it was seen fit to distinguish
at the state level between these powers, they must also be separate
at the federal level, and thus the US does NOT have the power
to make anything other than gold & silver coin legal tender.

What does all this mean to the average taxpayer?  The real
dollar in the US is the dollar coin, especially the silver dollar.
When you get paid, if you get paid in silver dollars, then you
can claim that you received $4,000 and pay taxes on that amount,
rather than counting $40,000 in federal reserve notes.  I've
checked with the IRS, and they will credit you with $1 in taxes
paid for each silver dollar you give them.  They cannot then
claim that a silver dollar is worth anthing other than $1.

So, to eliminate most taxes, an employer can start paying in
silver dollars, and the employees get the benefit.  You could
also sell a house or other asset for part federal reserve notes,
and part silver dollars, and thus make the official sale price
any amount you need to avoid capital gains tax.

Use this theory at your own risk, but in my mind it holds up
to scrutiny.

Dani Eder

