Date: Wed, 17 Feb 93 12:51:48 -0500
From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: BRAIN: uploading

Dave Cake writes:

>PS The problem with brute force uploading (simulating each individual neuron)
>is that we don't have anything like the scanning tech needed to completely 
>a neuron without removing it from the living brain. It is much more complicated
>than you might think (don't be fooled by overly simplistic neural network
> models).

So true.  Furthermore, we don't even know what the important elements are.
Neurons and synapses are probably not enough, you also need the complex
neurotransmitter relationships that are almost a complete mystery.  New
receptors, second messengers, feedback effects etc. are being discovered faster
than each is understood.  Next there are mysterious action potentials in glial
cells, suggesting that neurons may not be the only important processing
elements...

Then, there's the interactions with the environment, which some people
(including me) believe are crucial to the contextual framework of a mind.
Changing the sensory systems and the nature of one's experience might make it
all meaningless to the uploaded mind.  Without understanding the relationships
involved it is difficult to say what we could and could not change.

I don't believe that uploading is fundamentally impossible, but the optimism
about actually doing it should be put off for a few decades...

-Paul


