The focus of this issue of Leonardo Electronic News is New
Technology and Entertainment.  The reason for this is that I feel
that entertainment is an arena in which new technologies may be
gently introduced to the masses.   While thinking about content
for this issue, I went no further then my own back yard, the Bay
Area of San Francisco, California.  I still can't decide if I
should be apologetic about this or unabashedly proud because the
San Francisco Bay Area has a wonderfully diverse community of
artists, developers, filmmakers, musicians, and artistic
visionaries that selecting distinctly different themes within the
realm of technology and entertainment was effortless.

In the meantime I have just been making observations about how
new technologies are subliminally becoming commonplace.  For
instance, the February 5th issue of TIME Magazine's cover story
was about the "CYBERPUNK" movement, and among many other things,
how it is the place where science and art meet.  A television ad
for FEDERAL EXPRESS being run here features a toy that looks
conceivably like a Virtual Reality helmet and the two actors tout
the object as the hottest new thing from Japan.  I saw a cartoon,
a couple of weeks ago in the Sunday San Francisco
Chronicle/Examiner, where the main character falls into
Cyberspace and raves about its vast capabilities.  
MTV (the Music Television channel which has supposedly spawned
its own generation in the USA) recently profiled a 20 minute long
interactive movie that had a possible 68 outcomes.  There has
also been a lot of coverage about major periodicals are gearing
up for on-line and CD-Rom publications. The potential to enrich
our lives with technology is vast. 

The use of new technology is seeping into many entertainment
arenas. Ever since we first used machines for entertaining
people, there have been studies about the techniques and
technologies involved.  Now a myriad of computer programs,
robotics, imaging systems, animation techniques, sound recording,
and new ways of interacting in live performance are being used. 
New vocabularies for film, live performance, music/sound
recording, etc. are continually being developed and redefined. 
As a viewing audience we can either try to understand them or
just sit back and enjoy the show.                                

If you have information about exciting new technologies used for
creating art around the world, please forward them to me.  The
topic for the April 15 Leonardo Electronic News has not yet been
decided.  But I promise we will return to our well-rounded
international focus at that time.

Annie Lewis <fast@garnet.berkeley.edu>
