with virtuoso performers and artists, drawing on the individual
affections, talents, and interests of each. The Multimedia music
theater works created by George Coates, the artistic director
include: The Way of How; Are/Are; Seehear; Rare Area; Actual Sho;
Right Mind; The Architecture of Catastrophic Change; Invisible
Site; and The Desert Music: a live sho. Composers have included
Steve Reich, Paul Dresher and Marc Ream.

INVISIBLE SITE: A Virtual Sho played for many weeks in early 1992
at George Coates Performance Works(GCPW) permanent location which
is a former neo-Gothic cathedral in San Francisco, California.
This productions was a paramount attempt to combine live actors
with real-time animation.  

GCPW examined the world of virtual reality with this theatrical
piece.  INVISIBLE SITE featured computer animation, film clips
and 3-D slides projected onto a custom 30 by 60 foot perforated
aluminum screen.  The screen allowed for audiences to view both
the projected elements and live actors at behind it through 3-D
glasses, creating the illusion that they are interacting and
experiencing a sort of virtual reality.

Running the equipment for the show required nine back-stage
operators.  The company used its two Mac IIfx machines to create
both animation and music.  Many of the images were modeled by
MacroMind-Paracomp Inc.'s Swivel 3D software and animated in
Director, although they were parted via RIB (RenderMan Interface
Bytestream) format to a Silicon Graphics Iris 4D/320VGX
workstation  for faster playback.  Other animations were produced
with Kinetic Effects Inc.'s Life Forms software on both the Macs
and Iris.  The images are projected with an Esprit Projections
Systems 4000G graphics projector.

Composer Marc Ream wrote all the music using a Mac II, an EMAC 
CD-ROM player and a Digidesign NuBus card with Opcode Systems
Inc.s's Studio Interface to 10 synthesizers and drum machines. 
He used Opcode's Vision and Galaxy Plus Editors to sequence and
edit the music to digital audio tape.

