D'CUCKOO

NOTE: D'Cuckoo is spelled with an UMLAUT over the "u." 

D'CUCKOO (de koo+koo) is a techno-artist collective, a musical 
ensemble of women who are the world's only neo-classical,
post-industrial, cybertribal world-funk ensemble. D'Cuckoo
achieved international renown for their unique music and for the
innovative MIDI percussion instruments that they invented and
built. 

The members of D'Cuckoo have been working together since 
1985, composing and performing music while inventing and 
building their own digital musical instruments, unique, large-
scale electronic marimbas and "turtle" drum triggers. 

Before inventing these instruments, D'Cuckoo's co-founders 
built traditional acoustic marimbas and Japanese taiko drums, 
which they used in performance with the San Francisco 
Underground Marimba Ensemble (which incorporated South 
African Shona music from Zimbabwe) and San Francisco Taiko 
Dojo, which performs traditional and modern drumming rituals 
from Japan.

Today D'Cuckoo composes and performs a wide variety of 
musical styles, their repertoire influenced by African, Asian, 
pop, funk, and instrumental music. Through their unusual 
instruments they trigger an enormous range of custom digital 
sound and music samples. Their high-energy live performance 
is a multimedia event accentuated by live video and computer 
graphics, vivid slide projections, and active interaction with 
the audience, with the help of such interactive toys as the 
infamous D'Cuckoo MIDI Ball (featured on the Discovery Channel 
as well as NBC-TV news programs in 1992 and 1993). 

D'Cuckoo's instrumental and vocal performances are exotic, 
innovative, and surprising. Their music is characterized by 
engaging sonic depth and dynamic playing style. It's straight 
from the heart...yet their lyrics display the intellectual and 
political sensibilities of "one shared vision" and they never 
forget that music is founded upon rhythm.

They're called "D'Cuckoo" because they were told time and 
again that they were crazy for building their own instruments, 
ever since 1987, when they envisioned an electronic 
instrument that looked and played like a large, acoustic, 
African marimba and also triggered digital synthesizers and 
samplers. No one had ever built anything like that. So they 
decided to do it themselves, thinking: "If a synthesizer can 
convert signals from a piano-type keyboard into digital audio 
data, why can't we substitute a mallet-type keyboard? We 
could look like we're playing marimbas, but sound like 
anything." D'Cuckoo's one-of-a-kind electronic marimbas and 
drums now serve as digital music triggers, sounding like 
acoustic marimbas and drums, or a wide variety of other sound 
sources.

Because of their high-energy live shows featuring music-
controlled 3D computer graphics and the D'Cuckoo MidiBall, 
D'Cuckoo's been dubbed "the Grateful Dead of multimedia."

D'Cuckoo recently performed with Mickey Hart, Carlos Santana, 
Kitaro, and Steve Miller as part of "Mickey Hart & Friends" at 
Shoreline Amphitheater, outside of San Francisco, California.
This performance was part of "Our Colors: The Good Road Concert,"
a benefit for the Traditional Circle of Elders & Youth, a council
representing leadership for traditional indigenous peoples of
North America.

Last summer D'Cuckoo released D'Cuckoo, a CD/cassette on their
own Aisle of Women/D'Kayla label. The group is currently working
on three full-length album projects in their direct-to-disk
recording studio.

