PRIMAL BEHAVIOR PATTERNS

For the last three years in San Francisco, there has been a
collaboration under way.  Chico MacMurtrie's humanoid sculptures
whose muscles and bones are depicted by pneumatic cylinders and
metal, have fused with the brain synapses and neuron centers of
the computer hardware and programming of Rick Sayre and the
neuron team which includes George Homsy, Philip Robertson and
Frank Houseman. This group of people has come to be known as
AMORPHIC ROBOTICS.  

The obsession has been with depicting the most primal behavioral
patterns of the human condition, leading us to a better
understanding of the following behavior patterns: standing,
walking, falling, tumbling, throwing, drawing, playing music,
making love, eating/digesting/excreting food.  When these robots
are all brought together in the same space they make up a society
that  is unique but similar to our own.  A civilization of
primitives that mimic our basic behaviors, creating metaphors of
a society echoing the  anguish of a world deprived of the pure by
our dependence upon machines that we once controlled and that now
control us.

It is our attempt to create new situations that are as varied as
the individual audience member's primal reactions to the machines
we have brought to life.

One outcome of this collaboration, which also included composer
Bruce Darby was the December 1992 production and staging of
"Trigram: A Robotic Opera" at the Theatre Artaud, in San
Francisco, California.  The show was a mix of dance, theater,
video, film, computer graphics, technology and
kinetic/interactive art.

The main characters were approximately 20 mechanical musical
sculptures with 10 singer/musicians and 5 dancer/performers. 
Many of the robots were actually instruments who played
themselves. For example, the String Body which had a skeletal
metal frame, electric bass strings on an arm, upright bass
strings on a leg, a cello set up on its spine, two zithers for
its ribcage and a violin for its head.  When activated the robot
plucked its ribs with long fingers, bowed itself and tapped its
metallic head.  The string sounds were joined by the percussive
beats of pneumatics used to control its movements.  Other robotic
characters included the Drumming and Drawing Subhuman, Chime
Body. Horn Body, Drumming Bodies, The Morphic Cancerous Growth
Generating Devices, Overconsumption Figure, Urge to Stand Figure,
Visceral Body, and the Horny Skeletons.
