LEONARDO Joins The MIT Press

Since many of Leonardo Electronic News and FineArt Forum readers
may not be familiar with the other activities of Leonardo, the
International Society for the Arts, Sciences and Technology, we
are running a description of the journal Leonardo and our other
activities.

Beginning in 1993, The MIT Press will publish LEONARDO, a
scholarly bimonthly journal currently published by Pergamon
Press.  The journal is the official publication of Leonardo, The
International Society of the Arts, Sciences and Technology
(ISAST). 

Twenty-five years ago, LEONARDO was founded to provide an
international channel of communication between artists and others
interested in the arts, particularly those who use science and
developing technologies in their creations.  Today, LEONARDO is a
leading journal for anyone interested in the application of
contemporary science and technology to the arts and music.  It
currently reaches over 1,400 individuals and libraries worldwide.

LEONARDO focuses on interactions between the arts, sciences and 
technology.  The journal also covers media, music, kinetic art, 
performance art, language, environmental and conceptual art,
computers and artificial intelligence, and legal, economic, and
political aspects of art as these areas relate to the arts and/or
to the tools and ideas of contemporary science and technology.  

LEONARDO features editorials, illustrated articles by artists
writing about their own work, historical and theoretical
perspectives, reviews, technical articles, resource directories,
art/science forums, and music/sound technology explorations. 
Past articles include "Toward the Automatic Generation of Visual
Music," and "The Aesthetics of Viruses."  LEONARDO has presented
special issues on state-of-the-art developments: *Holography in
Art*, *Art and Telecommunications*, and *Visual Mathematics*.  

Since 1991, subscribers can also receive the companion annual
LEONARDO MUSIC JOURNAL, which comes with a sound CD and features
the latest in music, multimedia art, sound science, and
technology.  Additionally, Leonardo/ISAST publishes LEONARDO
ELECTRONIC NEWS, a monthly newsletter that reaches over 700
subscribers and is available on-line.

ABOUT THE EXECUTIVE EDITOR
Roger F. Malina, son of LEONARDO's founding editor, Frank J.
Malina, is an astronomer at the Center for Extreme Ultraviolet
Astrophysics, University of California, Berkeley, and an author
on art and technology issues.

